Architecting the Civilizational Immune System

Formal Safety Guarantees for Lunar Habitat Life‑Support under Delays and Faults
—Revised Edition—

Ouadi Maakoul
Advisors: Gemini, Claude , Deepseek 

---

Abstract

We present a layered formal framework for guaranteeing the safe operation of life‑support control systems in a permanently crewed lunar habitat. The approach combines (i) a metric temporal logic specification language with an explicit Earth–Moon communication latency, (ii) a discrete‑time Control Barrier Function (CBF) synthesis that enforces those specifications under actuator limits and power budgets, (iii) a machine‑checked inductive‑invariant proof of the closed‑loop system in the Isabelle/HOL proof assistant, (iv) compositional assume‑guarantee contracts with a non‑circular induction that resolves genuine circular dependencies among subsystems, and (v) a quantitative reliability analysis that bounds the residual failure probability from single‑event upsets when the control logic is implemented on radiation‑hardened hardware with triple modular redundancy and an independent fail‑safe ROM.

The work itself embodies the culture of argumentation advocated by contemporary research in mathematics education: every non‑trivial proof is either machine‑checked or subjected to peer‑review‑style scrutiny, the development followed an iterative brainstorm–sketch–revise process, and the use of proof assistants served as an objective check on logical gaps. The safety guarantee is deterministic under nominal hardware (proved in Isabelle/HOL) and probabilistic ( < 10⁻⁹ over ten years) when physical faults are considered. All claims are scoped carefully: we identify which results are formally proved, which rely on verified assumptions, and which remain open. The dissertation aims to provide a rigorous, human‑auditable, and machine‑checkable foundation for certifying autonomous life‑critical systems in deep‑space environments where remote human intervention is impossible within the timescale of emergencies.

