1. Introduction: The Civilizational Immune System and a Culture of Rigour

1.1 The Problem

A permanent human settlement on the Moon must maintain a habitable atmosphere, water, and thermal environment without real‑time ground intervention during emergencies. The one‑way Earth–Moon signal delay of approximately 1.3 s, combined with round‑trip control latencies of 2.6 s, forces the habitat to rely on on‑board autonomous safety controllers that can enforce survival constraints within a fraction of a control cycle (here taken as 0.2 s). The system must also withstand cosmic‑ray induced bit‑flips that can corrupt safety‑critical computations.

We conceptualise this layered protection as a “civilisational immune system”: a hierarchy of formal specifications, control‑theoretic invariance proofs, and fault‑tolerant hardware that collectively ensure the habitat never enters a lethal state. To be trustworthy, this immune system must itself be built from mathematical arguments that are, in the words of modern mathematics education research, transparent, open to critique, and machine‑checkable wherever possible. Our work is therefore as much an exercise in how to structure such a proof culture as it is a concrete proposal for a lunar habitat.

1.2 A Culture of Argumentation and Proof

We deliberately adopted the principles advocated by the contemporary “transition‑to‑proof” movement and the brainstorm‑sketch‑revise cycle (see, e.g., the 2023–2026 arXiv literature on proof education). Specifically:

· Every safety‑critical proof is either formalised in Isabelle/HOL (Parts III and IV) or reduced to elementary inequalities that have been peer‑reviewed by multiple advisors and simulated extensively.
· The L‑MTL logic was not presented as a theoretical novelty but as a traceable convenience over standard Metric Temporal Logic, and its expressivity claims were examined critically with the same rigour one applies when validating a proof. We retracted an earlier claim of strict subsumption after peer review, illustrating the sociomathematical norm of consensus‑building through critique.
· The CBF synthesis and the compositional contracts were developed in an iterative fashion: we first sketched barrier functions and contracts, formalised the key invariance argument in Isabelle, discovered a circular dependency that required a non‑circular induction step, and then revised the contracts and the proof accordingly. This mirrors the brainstorm‑sketch‑revise process that professional mathematicians use.
· Wherever possible, we use faded worked examples in the presentation: we first state a complete theorem, then in subsequent sections we gradually remove parts of the proof so that the reader (and our future selves) must fill in the missing logical steps, thereby building deep understanding.
· We emphasise proof validation over mere proof generation. In Parts III and IV, the Isabelle/HOL formalisation acts as an objective validator, forcing us to confront every hidden assumption.

1.3 Contributions

1. Lunar Metric Temporal Logic (L‑MTL) – a definitional extension of MTL with an explicit one‑way delay parameter, plus macros for lunar night and low‑power phases. All formulas translate into MTL, so existing model‑checking algorithms apply. The logic serves as a specification language, not a new decidability domain.
2. Discrete‑Time Control Barrier Functions (DT‑CBFs) – we synthesise DT‑CBFs that enforce L‑MTL safety formulas for the coupled oxygen–thermal–power subsystem. The synthesis is manual but systematic; we prove feasibility under actuator and power constraints using explicit numerical bounds and a convex QP formulation.
3. Machine‑checked inductive invariant – in Isabelle/HOL we prove that any system whose controller satisfies the CBF condition preserves the safe set forever. The formalisation abstracts over the plant dynamics and the controller, treating them as functions meeting a contract, thereby separating the control‑theoretic proof from the implementation.
4. Compositional assume‑guarantee contracts – we show that the oxygen generation, thermal control, and power management subsystems can be verified separately. The contracts exhibit genuine circularity, which we resolve with a discrete‑time induction that uses only past guarantees to infer current assumptions. The circularity is thus rendered logically sound.
5. Hardware reliability – we quantify the probability that radiation‑induced upsets defeat the CBF filter, and design a two‑level triple modular redundancy (TMR) scheme together with a fail‑safe ROM. We show that the overall mission risk is below 10⁻⁹ over ten years, and we stress that this bound remains valid even under significantly relaxed upset‑rate assumptions.
6. Limitations – we explicitly list the assumptions, idealisations, and open problems (e.g., state estimation under delays, real‑time scheduling, full automation of CBF synthesis) to prevent over‑claiming, in keeping with the mathematical culture of acknowledging the boundaries of one’s proofs.

1.4 Structure

Parts I–V develop each component; Part VI unifies them into an end‑to‑end theorem; Part VII discusses limitations and future work. Throughout, we adopt a writing style that makes the logical dependencies explicit, and we include remarks on why certain proof steps are valid, not merely how they are carried out.

