Part I – A Metric Temporal Logic with Explicit Communication Delay

I.1 Primitives

Definition 1.1 (Timed State Sequence). Let \mathcal{S} \subset \mathbb{R}^n be a compact, connected state space and \text{AP} a finite set of atomic propositions. A timed state sequence is an infinite sequence \rho = (s_0, t_0)(s_1, t_1)\cdots with s_k \in \mathcal{S}, t_0 = 0, and t_{k+1} > t_k. A labelling function L : \mathcal{S} \to 2^{\text{AP}} is induced by sub‑level sets: p \in L(s) \iff g_p(s) \ge 0 for a continuously differentiable g_p with non‑zero gradient on the boundary.

Definition 1.2 (Lunar Communication Delay). The Earth–Moon one‑way signal propagation time is fixed at \delta_0 = 1.3\,\text{s}. Any ground intervention can affect the habitat state only at least \delta_0 after detection.

I.2 Syntax and Semantics of L‑MTL

L‑MTL is a syntactic extension of Metric Temporal Logic (MTL) with a built‑in delay parameter and convenient macros for night and power modes.

Definition 1.3 (L‑MTL Formulas).

\phi ::= \top \mid \bot \mid p \mid \neg\phi \mid \phi_1 \land \phi_2 \mid \mathbf{X}\phi \mid \phi_1 \mathbf{U}^{\delta}_{I} \phi_2 \mid \mathbf{G}_{\text{night}}\phi \mid \mathbf{F}_{\text{power}}\phi

where p \in \text{AP}, I = [a,b] is a closed interval with 0 \le a < b < \infty, and \delta \ge 0 is a constant (for lunar operations \delta = \delta_0; set \delta = 0 for purely local reasoning). Derived operators are defined as usual.

Definition 1.4 (Night and Low‑Power Macros).
\mathcal{N}(\rho) = \{k \mid \text{Sun occluded by Earth at } t_k\} (determined by lunar ephemeris);
\mathcal{P}(\rho) = \{k \mid P_{\text{elec}}(s_k) < P_{\text{thresh}}\}.

Then:
(\rho,k) \models \mathbf{G}_{\text{night}}\phi iff for all j \ge k with j \in \mathcal{N}(\rho), (\rho,j) \models \phi.
(\rho,k) \models \mathbf{F}_{\text{power}}\phi iff there exists j \ge k with j \in \mathcal{P}(\rho) and (\rho,j) \models \phi.

These macros expand into finite conjunctions/disjunctions for bounded mission lengths, as detailed in the proof of translation into MTL (Lemma I.2).

The semantics of the delay‑until is defined as:

(\rho,k) \models \phi_1 \mathbf{U}^{\delta}_{[a,b]} \phi_2 \quad \text{iff} \quad \exists j \ge k : t_j - t_k \in [a,\, b+\delta],\; (\rho,j) \models \phi_2,\; \forall i \in [k,j): (\rho,i) \models \phi_1.

When \delta = 0 this reduces to standard MTL \mathbf{U}_{[a,b]}. The extra \delta in the upper bound accounts for the worst‑case latency of an Earth command.

Remark I.1 (Encoding into MTL). Any L‑MTL formula \phi can be translated into an equivalent MTL formula \phi^* by replacing every \mathbf{U}^{\delta}_{[a,b]} with \mathbf{U}_{[a,b+\delta]} and adjusting the other operators accordingly. Conversely, MTL is the fragment of L‑MTL with \delta = 0. Therefore L‑MTL is a definitional extension, not a strictly more expressive logic. We introduce the separate parameter \delta to make the physical latency explicit in the specification, which aids traceability and human review.

Theorem I.1 (Translation Correctness). For every timed state sequence \rho and every L‑MTL formula \phi, (\rho,0) \models \phi iff (\rho,0) \models \phi^*, where \phi^* is the MTL translation.

Proof. By structural induction on \phi. The only non‑trivial case is the delay‑until operator, which follows directly from the definition. ∎

Corollary I.2. Since MTL over finite timed words with bounded variability is decidable (Ouaknine & Worrell 2006), the same holds for L‑MTL specifications of bounded‑length missions. For our discrete‑time control system with sampling period T_s = 0.2 s, the semantics is naturally discrete, avoiding undecidability concerns.

I.3 A Proof System (Sketch)

We present a Hilbert‑style axiomatisation \mathbf{H} for L‑MTL that extends standard MTL axioms. Because L‑MTL is a definitional extension, completeness for the MTL fragment carries over; a full completeness proof would follow the lines of MTL completeness results but is not attempted here. For our purposes, the proof system is used only for deductive verification of simple properties, and we rely on model checking for automated verification.

Theorem I.3 (Soundness). Every provable formula is valid.
Proof. Routine induction on the length of derivation. ∎

We leave completeness as an open problem, but stress that the safety fragment we need is well‑covered by MTL decision procedures.

I.4 Expressiveness Discussion

The original manuscript claimed strict subsumption of MTL. That claim has been retracted after careful peer review, exemplifying the sociomathematical norm of consensus building. L‑MTL is a convenience layer over MTL; the delay parameter makes explicit a physical constant that would otherwise be hidden inside numeric interval bounds. All subsequent sections treat L‑MTL formulas as MTL formulas with enlarged intervals.

