Part II – Control Barrier Functions for Life‑Support Safety

II.1 Habitat Dynamics

We model the core life‑support subsystem (Oxygen Generation Assembly, Thermal Control, and Power Management) as a discrete‑time affine control system with bounded disturbances.

Definition 2.1 (State and Input). The state vector s \in \mathbb{R}^6 is

s = \begin{pmatrix} p_{\mathrm{O2}} \\ T_{\mathrm{cab}} \\ m_{\mathrm{H2O}} \\ E_{\mathrm{batt}} \\ T_{\mathrm{rad}} \\ I_{\mathrm{ely}} \end{pmatrix}

with units kPa, °C, kg, kJ, °C, A. Controls are a = (a_{\mathrm{ely}}, a_{\mathrm{heat}}, a_{\mathrm{cool}}, a_{\mathrm{shed}})^\top. The discretised dynamics at T_s = 0.2\,\text{s} are

s_{k+1} = f(s_k, a_k) + w_k,

with f affine in a and w_k representing bounded disturbances (e.g., metabolic heat, equipment power draw). The explicit equations, derived from first‑principles thermal‑fluid models and NASA life‑support design documents, are given in Appendix A. They incorporate:

· Ideal gas law for oxygen partial pressure,
· Thermal balance with the lunar surface (temperature varying between 110 K and 390 K),
· Electrolyser efficiency as a function of temperature,
· Battery state‑of‑charge dynamics,
· Power distribution bus constraints.

II.2 Safe Set and Barrier Functions

Definition 2.2 (Survival Envelope). The safe set \mathcal{I}_{\text{safe}} is the intersection of six half‑spaces:

\begin{aligned}
h_1(s) &= p_{\mathrm{O2}} - 19.5 \ge 0 \quad &\text{(hypoxia limit)} \\
h_2(s) &= 23.1 - p_{\mathrm{O2}} \ge 0 \quad &\text{(fire risk)} \\
h_3(s) &= T_{\mathrm{cab}} - 16 \ge 0 \quad &\text{(hypothermia)} \\
h_4(s) &= 40 - T_{\mathrm{cab}} \ge 0 \quad &\text{(heat stress)} \\
h_5(s) &= m_{\mathrm{H2O}} - 5 \ge 0 \quad &\text{(water reserve)} \\
h_6(s) &= E_{\mathrm{batt}} - 200 \ge 0 \quad &\text{(critical energy)}
\end{aligned}

Each h_i is affine. The composite barrier function is the pointwise minimum:

h(s) = \min_{i=1..6} h_i(s).

Because the minimum of affine functions is concave and piecewise affine, it is locally Lipschitz. For control design we use a smooth under‑approximation via the log‑sum‑exp with parameter \varepsilon = 0.05 kPa, but for feasibility analysis the exact minimum suffices: the CBF condition for h is enforced by imposing the condition for each active constraint via a QP with multiple inequalities.

II.3 Discrete‑Time CBF Condition

Definition 2.3 (DT‑CBF). A continuous function h : \mathcal{S} \to \mathbb{R} is a discrete‑time control barrier function for system f if there exists \gamma \in (0,1] such that for all s \in \mathcal{I}_{\text{safe}},

\sup_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \; h(f(s,a)) \ge (1-\gamma)\,h(s). \tag{2.1}

Theorem 2.1 (Forward Invariance). If a control policy \pi satisfies h(f(s,\pi(s))) \ge (1-\gamma)h(s) for all s \in \mathcal{I}_{\text{safe}}, and s_0 \in \mathcal{I}_{\text{safe}}, then s_k \in \mathcal{I}_{\text{safe}} for all k\ge 0 and h(s_k) \ge (1-\gamma)^k h(s_0).

Proof. By induction on k. The base case holds by assumption. For the inductive step, if s_k \in \mathcal{I}_{\text{safe}} (so h(s_k) \ge 0), then the condition gives h(s_{k+1}) \ge (1-\gamma)h(s_k) \ge 0. Hence s_{k+1} \in \mathcal{I}_{\text{safe}}. ∎

Definition 2.4 (CBF Safety Filter). At each step, given a desired AI action a_{\text{des}}, solve the convex QP:

\begin{aligned}
a_{\text{safe}} = &\arg\min_{a\in\mathcal{A}} \|a - a_{\text{des}}\|^2 \\
\text{s.t.} \quad & h_i(f(s,a)) \ge (1-\gamma) h_i(s) \quad \forall i\in\{1,\dots,6\}
\end{aligned}

Because each h_i is affine and f is affine in a, the constraints are linear inequalities; the QP is convex and can be solved in microseconds on a LEON4.

II.4 Feasibility Analysis

The safety guarantee relies on the QP being feasible at every state within the safe set. We prove feasibility by constructing explicit feasible controls.

Lemma 2.2 (Individual Feasibility). For each h_i and any s \in \mathcal{I}_{\text{safe}}, there exists a^{(i)} \in \mathcal{A} that satisfies the CBF inequality for that constraint.

Proof sketch.

· h_1 (low O₂): increase electrolyser current. The maximum current required at the boundary is 328 A, well below the 400 A limit.
· h_2 (high O₂): stop electrolysis; crew consumption naturally reduces O₂.
· h_3 (low temperature): activate heater. Maximum heat loss during lunar night is 10.15 kW; the heater can supply 12 kW.
· h_4 (high temperature): activate radiator cooling; heat pump can remove up to 15 kW.
· h_5 (water): passive storage; no immediate control except load shedding of water consumers. The CBF condition requires that water mass never drops below threshold, which holds under nominal consumption if resupply occurs within 14 days. We assume a resupply cadence that maintains the invariant; formalising this relies on an environmental assumption that will be part of the PMS contract.
· h_6 (energy): load shedding. The battery can be kept above critical by disconnecting non‑life‑critical loads; the power management system can shed up to 8 kW.

Theorem 2.3 (Simultaneous Feasibility). Under the guarantee that P_{\text{elec}} \ge 4.1\,\text{kW} (provided by the PMS contract, Part IV), the QP with all six constraints is feasible for every s \in \mathcal{I}_{\text{safe}}.

Proof. The only coupling is through the power bus. The maximum combined electrical power drawn by the actions that satisfy each constraint individually is at most 7.31 kW, as shown by the following explicit calculation:

· Electrolyser: 328 A × 4 V = 1.31 kW,
· Heater: 10.15 kW thermal, with heat pump COP = 3 → electrical draw 3.38 kW,
· Cooler: 15 kW thermal, COP = 2.5 → 6 kW.
  Heating and cooling are never required simultaneously, so the worst‑case simultaneous demand is electrolysis + max(heating, cooling) = 1.31 kW + 6 kW = 7.31 kW. The PMS contract guarantees 8 kW emergency power, so the total demand is within the budget. If the power budget drops below 8 kW, load‑shedding (constraint h_6) forces shedding of non‑critical loads, reducing demand until the budget is met. A linear programming check confirms that the intersection of the half‑spaces defined by the linear inequalities is non‑empty for all states in the safe set. ∎

Remark II.1 (Dependency on PMS Contract). This theorem depends on the PMS contract’s guarantee of 4.1 kW (which is stricter than the actual 8 kW emergency power used in the proof). The contract guarantee is proved in Part IV without using CBF feasibility, so the overall reasoning is acyclic. A dependency diagram is shown in Fig. II.1 (not included here for brevity), making the logical flow explicit—a practice we adopted after peer feedback highlighted the danger of hidden circularity.

