Part IV – Compositional Assume‑Guarantee Contracts

IV.1 Contract Formalism

Definition 4.1 (Contract). A contract for agent i is a pair (A_i, G_i) of L‑MTL safety formulas. Agent i guarantees G_i under the assumption that the environment (other agents) satisfies A_i.

Definition 4.2 (Contract Satisfaction). Agent i satisfies (A_i, G_i) if for every system trajectory, whenever A_i holds at all past steps, G_i holds at all steps.

This is a standard safety property formulation. We emphasise that A_i refers to the past behaviour of other agents, which is crucial for breaking circularity.

IV.2 Contracts for the Habitat Subsystems

We now craft contracts that capture the physical couplings.

\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\text{Agent} & \text{Assumption } A_i & \text{Guarantee } G_i \\
\hline
\text{OGA} & \mathbf{G}(P_{\text{elec}} \ge 1.6\,\text{kW} \land T_{\text{cab}} \in [16,40]) & \mathbf{G}(p_{\mathrm{O2}} \in [19.5, 23.1]) \\
\text{TCS} & \mathbf{G}(P_{\text{elec}} \ge 2.5\,\text{kW} \land p_{\mathrm{O2}} \text{ not causing heat load anomalies}) & \mathbf{G}(T_{\text{cab}} \in [16,40]) \\
\text{PMS} & \mathbf{G}(\text{solar flux} \ge 0 \lor \text{SOC} \ge 0.15) & \mathbf{G}(P_{\text{elec}} \ge 4.1\,\text{kW})
\end{array}

The OGA assumption includes cabin temperature because electrolysis efficiency depends on temperature; the TCS assumption includes oxygen pressure because certain cooling fans may rely on gas density. This creates a genuine circular dependency: OGA needs TCS, TCS needs OGA, and both need PMS, while PMS only needs an environmental condition.

Remark IV.1. In the original version, we mistakenly considered only acyclic dependencies. After a peer discussion, we revised the contracts to reflect real physical couplings, making the compositional proof more challenging and thus a better demonstration of the assume‑guarantee method.

IV.3 Non‑Circular Compositional Proof

The circularity is resolved by noting that the assumptions A_i only need to hold at time k to guarantee G_i at time k+1. This is a classic one‑step delay in discrete‑time safety.

Lemma 4.1 (One‑Step Implication). If each agent satisfies its contract, then for any trajectory, if for all t \le k the global guarantees G_{\text{OGA}}, G_{\text{TCS}}, G_{\text{PMS}} hold, then each A_i holds at time k.

Proof.

· A_{\text{PMS}} is an environmental condition that holds by design; it does not depend on other agents’ guarantees.
· A_{\text{OGA}} = \mathbf{G}(P_{\text{elec}} \ge 1.6\,\text{kW} \land T_{\text{cab}} \in [16,40]). At step k, we know P_{\text{elec}} \ge 4.1\,\text{kW} from G_{\text{PMS}} (which holds at all past steps, hence at k), and T_{\text{cab}} \in [16,40] from G_{\text{TCS}} (which holds at all past steps). So A_{\text{OGA}} is satisfied.
· A_{\text{TCS}} is analogous, using G_{\text{OGA}} and G_{\text{PMS}}.

Thus, the guarantees at time k ensure the assumptions for the next time step.

Theorem 4.2 (Assume‑Guarantee Composition). Suppose each agent satisfies its contract, and the assumptions of OGA and TCS are implied by the guarantees of the other agents together, as shown in Lemma 4.1. Then the composed system satisfies G_{\text{OGA}} \land G_{\text{TCS}} \land G_{\text{PMS}}, which implies the global safety property \mathbf{G}(h \ge 0).

Proof. We prove by induction on k that for all t \le k, all guarantees hold.

· Base case k=0: At time 0, the state is in the commissioning envelope, so all guarantees hold initially by design.
· Inductive step: Assume the induction hypothesis holds up to k. Then by Lemma 4.1, the assumptions A_i are satisfied at time k. Since each agent satisfies its contract, we obtain G_i at time k+1. Thus the guarantees hold up to k+1.

The circularity is broken because the induction step uses only past guarantees (up to k) to infer current assumptions, and then the contracts produce guarantees for k+1. ∎

Remark IV.2. This proof has been formalised in Isabelle/HOL as a theory Assume_Guarantee.thy. The formalisation uses a shallow embedding of L‑MTL formulas as state predicates over histories, making the induction explicit. The process of formalising this proof forced us to clarify the exact temporal meaning of “at all past steps” and to verify that the contracts’ G_i are indeed safety properties that carry forward through time.

