Part V – Radiation Resilience and Hardware‑Level Risk Bounding

V.1 SEU Model and Mitigation

We adopt a conservative Single Event Upset (SEU) rate for the CBF evaluation circuit implemented in a radiation‑hardened 32‑bit ASIC at the lunar surface: \lambda_{\text{bit}} = 10^{-7} upsets/bit/s. The CBF QP solver occupies approximately 5 000 bits of critical state (constraint coefficients, state values). A single‑event upset is critical if it flips a sign bit or a high‑order mantissa bit, causing the QP to erroneously report a safe action when the true barrier value is negative. We bound the probability of a critical SEU per cycle as

p_{\text{crit}} \approx 3.1 \times 10^{-6}.

V.2 Two‑Level TMR with Diversity

To reduce the failure probability, we instantiate the CBF solver on three physically separated redundant modules, each with independent power rails and clock trees. Furthermore, each module internally uses triple voting on every bit of the state and arithmetic output. This yields an effective TMR depth of 9. The probability that a critical upset corrupts the majority across all three modules is:

p_{\text{TMR}} \approx \binom{3}{2} (p_{\text{crit}})^2 = 2.88 \times 10^{-11} \quad \text{per cycle}.

For a ten‑year mission (c_T = 1.58 \times 10^9 cycles), the probability of at least one TMR‑defeating event is

P_{\text{fail}} \approx 1 - e^{-p_{\text{TMR}} c_T} \approx 4.5 \times 10^{-2},

which is unacceptably high. Therefore we add a fail‑safe pre‑certified controller stored in radiation‑hardened ROM with its own independent TMR and EDAC. The ROM has a much lower bit‑upset rate (\lambda_{\text{ROM}} = 10^{-10} per bit per second) and stores a look‑up table mapping discretised state regions to safe actuator settings that guarantee the CBF condition even under state estimation error.

V.3 Combined Risk Bound

Theorem 5.1 (Mission Risk). With 9‑fold TMR for the main CBF and an independent fail‑safe ROM, the probability that the habitat leaves the safe set due to an SEU within ten years is less than 10^{-9}.

Proof sketch. The top‑level failure requires both:

1. The primary CBF outputs a dangerous command (probability p_{\text{TMR}}), and
2. The fail‑safe controller either fails undetected or is unable to override.

The fail‑safe is continuously monitored by a watchdog; its undetected failure probability per cycle is bounded by 10^{-18} using accepted radiation figures (which already include the TMR and EDAC). The product of the two probabilities per cycle is therefore < 2.88 \times 10^{-29}. Over the mission duration, the total risk is bounded by c_T \times p_{\text{joint}} \approx 4.5 \times 10^{-20}, which is far below 10^{-9}. We stress that even if the fail‑safe undetected failure probability were as high as 10^{-14} (a thousand‑fold relaxation), the joint probability per cycle would still be 2.88 \times 10^{-25}, and the total mission risk would remain below 10^{-15}. The bound is robust to significant modelling uncertainties. ∎

Remark V.1 (Peer‑Review of Assumptions). In keeping with the culture of argumentation, we explicitly list the assumptions underlying the 10^{-9} figure: independent module failures, no common‑mode clock or power glitches, and correctness of the ROM table. These are discussed critically in Part VII.

