Part VI – Integration Theorem and End‑to‑End Certificate

Theorem 6.1 (End‑to‑End Safety Certificate). Consider the lunar habitat with the coupled OGA‑TCS‑PMS system satisfying the contracts of Part IV, each controlled by the CBF safety filter from Part II, implemented on the fault‑tolerant hardware of Part V. If the initial state is within the commissioning envelope, then:

1. Deterministic guarantee: In the absence of hardware faults, the habitat state remains in \mathcal{I}_{\text{safe}} for all time.
2. Probabilistic guarantee: Accounting for SEUs, the probability that a fault causes a safety violation within ten years is less than 10^{-9}.

Proof. Part 1 follows from the assume‑guarantee composition (Theorem 4.2), the CBF invariance (Theorem 2.1), and the feasibility of the QP (Theorem 2.3). Part 2 follows from the hardware reliability analysis (Theorem 5.1) together with the fact that any undetected fault in the CBF logic is caught by the fail‑safe. The end‑to‑end certificate is thus established by threading the individual proofs together, ensuring that the dependencies are acyclic (see dependency diagram in Appendix D). ∎

