Part VII – Limitations, Assumptions, and Future Work

No formal method can eliminate all risk; the value of a dissertation lies as much in delineating the boundaries of its guarantees as in the guarantees themselves. In the spirit of the brainstorm‑sketch‑revise process, we treat this section as a living document, subject to continuous peer feedback.

7.1 Assumptions and Idealizations

· State estimation: We assumed perfect, zero‑delay state measurement. In reality, sensors have noise and latency. Future work must incorporate an observer and robust CBF conditions (input‑to‑state stable CBFs).
· Computational real‑time guarantees: The QP must be solved in < 0.2 s on a LEON4. While convex QP solvers can be certified for worst‑case execution time, we have not performed a timing analysis. This is a critical gap that must be closed with a WCET‑certified solver.
· L‑MTL completeness: We have only sketched a proof system; a full completeness result is left open. However, for our safety fragment, the translation to MTL and existing decision procedures suffice.
· CBF synthesis automation: Our barrier functions were manually crafted. Compiling temporal logic specifications into feasible CBFs automatically remains an open area.
· Radiation assumptions: We assumed independent failures across TMR modules. Common‑mode failures (e.g., power surges, clock glitches) could defeat TMR. More robust designs use diverse redundancy. Our ROM reliability numbers are optimistic and need experimental validation.
· Lunar environment uncertainties: The thermal model uses simplified boundary conditions; dust accumulation, variable albedo, and equipment degradation are not modelled.
· Scalability: We handled a 6‑state system with 3 agents. Scaling to a full habitat will require automated contract generation and compositional verification tools.

7.2 Open Problems and Future Directions

· Formalised CBF synthesis: Extend the Isabelle/HOL formalisation to include the QP feasibility proof, thereby closing the gap between the abstract invariance theorem and the concrete controller.
· Real‑time scheduling integration: Combine the CBF filter with a real‑time OS model to guarantee that the QP computation always meets its deadline. This would likely involve a co‑verification of timing and safety properties in a proof assistant.
· Sensor‑noise robustness: Develop an ISS‑CBF formulation and prove that the invariant still holds with bounded sensor errors, possibly using the compositional framework to treat sensing as an agent with its own contract.
· Full L‑MTL decision procedure: While the safety fragment is decidable, a complete decision procedure for the entire L‑MTL language (or its MTL translation) over discrete‑time sequences would strengthen the specification layer.
· Integration with mission‑level planning: The CBF safety filter currently operates at the reactive level. Integrating it with a deliberative planner that respects the same temporal logic contracts would enable a layered autonomy architecture.

7.3 Reflection on the Proof Culture

Throughout this work, we strove to adhere to the principles of modern proof pedagogy: we brainstormed ideas on a whiteboard, sketched proofs on paper, subjected them to peer critique, and revised them iteratively. The machine‑checked Isabelle/HOL proofs serve as an objective standard, akin to the “autograding” tools used in proof education. By making our logical dependencies explicit and our limitations transparent, we hope to contribute not only to space systems engineering but also to a broader culture of rigorous, trustworthy autonomous systems design. Future work should continue to merge the worlds of formal verification and control theory with the same critical, collaborative spirit.



Appendix A — Habitat Dynamics: First‑Principles Model and Linearisation

The state vector s\in\mathbb{R}^6 and control vector a\in\mathbb{R}^4 are as defined in Section II.1. The continuous‑time dynamics are derived from conservation laws and constitutive relations. We discretise with a zero‑order hold and sampling period T_s = 0.2\,\text{s}.

A.1 Oxygen Partial Pressure

The cabin atmosphere is modelled as an ideal gas at constant volume V_{\text{cab}} = 120\;\text{m}^3 and temperature T_{\text{cab}}. Oxygen partial pressure evolves according to

\frac{d p_{\mathrm{O2}}}{dt} = \frac{R T_{\text{cab}}}{V_{\text{cab}}} \bigl( \dot n_{\text{in}} - \dot n_{\text{out}} \bigr),

where R = 8.314\;\text{J·mol}^{-1}\text{K}^{-1}. The molar inflow \dot n_{\text{in}} is proportional to electrolyser current I_{\mathrm{ely}} via Faraday’s law:

\dot n_{\text{in}} = \frac{\eta_F(I_{\mathrm{ely}}, T_{\text{cab}})}{4F}\, I_{\mathrm{ely}},
\quad
\eta_F = \eta_0 \bigl(1 - \alpha(T_{\text{cab}} - T_{\text{ref}})\bigr),

with F = 96485\;\text{C·mol}^{-1}, \eta_0 = 0.95, \alpha = 0.002\;\text{K}^{-1}, T_{\text{ref}} = 298\;\text{K}. The oxygen consumption by crew (four persons at 0.84\;\text{kg·day}^{-1} each) gives a constant molar outflow \dot n_{\text{out}} = 1.22 \times 10^{-3}\;\text{mol·s}^{-1}. During electrolyser shutdown, a backup oxygen supply can inject up to 1.0\;\text{mol·s}^{-1}, modelled as a bounded disturbance.

A.2 Cabin Thermal Balance

The cabin temperature T_{\text{cab}} satisfies

C_{\text{cab}} \frac{d T_{\text{cab}}}{dt} = \dot Q_{\text{int}} + \dot Q_{\text{solar}} - \dot Q_{\text{rad}} + \dot Q_{\text{heat}} - \dot Q_{\text{cool}},

where C_{\text{cab}} = 400\;\text{kJ·K}^{-1} is the effective thermal capacity. Internal gains \dot Q_{\text{int}} = 2.0\;\text{kW} (crew, electronics). Solar load through windows \dot Q_{\text{solar}} = \alpha_s A_{\text{win}} G_s, with \alpha_s=0.3, A_{\text{win}}=2\;\text{m}^2, G_s\in[0,1360]\;\text{W·m}^{-2} depending on lunar day/night. Radiative loss to the lunar surface at temperature T_{\text{surf}}\in[110,390]\;\text{K}:

\dot Q_{\text{rad}} = \varepsilon \sigma A_{\text{rad}} (T_{\text{cab}}^4 - T_{\text{surf}}^4),

with \varepsilon=0.9, \sigma = 5.67\times 10^{-8}\;\text{W·m}^{-2}\text{K}^{-4}, A_{\text{rad}} = 10\;\text{m}^2. The heater power \dot Q_{\text{heat}} = \text{COP}_h P_{\text{heat}} where P_{\text{heat}} is electrical power, \text{COP}_h = 3.0; similarly \dot Q_{\text{cool}} = \text{COP}_c P_{\text{cool}} with \text{COP}_c = 2.5. Actuator limits: P_{\text{heat}} \le 4\;\text{kW}, P_{\text{cool}} \le 6\;\text{kW}.

A.3 Water Mass Balance

Water is produced by fuel cells and consumed by crew. The mass balance is

\frac{d m_{\mathrm{H2O}}}{dt} = \dot m_{\text{prod}} - \dot m_{\text{cons}},

with \dot m_{\text{cons}} = 3.36\;\text{kg·day}^{-1} (four crew) and \dot m_{\text{prod}} proportional to electrolyser current (assuming H₂/O₂ fuel cell recombination). This dynamics is slow and considered passively safe; the CBF constraint h_5 enforces a lower bound.

A.4 Battery Energy

The battery state of charge is represented by stored energy E_{\text{batt}} (kJ):

\frac{d E_{\text{batt}}}{dt} = P_{\text{charge}} - P_{\text{discharge}} - P_{\text{self}},

where P_{\text{charge}} is from solar arrays, P_{\text{discharge}} is the total electrical load, and P_{\text{self}} = 0.01 E_{\text{batt}} models self‑discharge. The power bus constraint is

P_{\text{elec}} = P_{\text{solar}} - P_{\text{charge}} \ge 0,

and P_{\text{elec}} supplies all loads. The battery capacity is E_{\text{max}} = 10\,000\;\text{kJ}; the critical threshold is 200\;\text{kJ}.

A.5 Radiator Temperature and Electrolyser Current Dynamics

The radiator temperature T_{\text{rad}} is governed by a first‑order lag:

\tau_{\text{rad}} \frac{d T_{\text{rad}}}{dt} = T_{\text{cab}} - T_{\text{rad}},
\quad \tau_{\text{rad}} = 120\;\text{s}.

The electrolyser current I_{\mathrm{ely}} obeys a similar lag from the set‑point a_{\mathrm{ely}}:

\tau_{\text{ely}} \frac{d I_{\mathrm{ely}}}{dt} = a_{\mathrm{ely}} - I_{\mathrm{ely}},
\quad \tau_{\text{ely}} = 0.5\;\text{s}.

A.6 Discrete‑Time Affine Model

Integrating over T_s with zero‑order hold on controls and first‑order Euler approximation yields the discrete‑time model s_{k+1} = A s_k + B a_k + d_k, where A\in\mathbb{R}^{6\times 6}, B\in\mathbb{R}^{6\times 4} are constant matrices and d_k captures bounded disturbances from metabolic loads, solar irradiance, and lunar surface temperature. The exact entries of A,B are omitted for brevity but are linearisations of the above equations around the nominal operating point. Importantly, the map is affine in the control a_k; thus f(s,a) is affine in a.

