Appendix C — Radiation Reliability Calculations: Detailed Bounds

We provide the step‑by‑step derivation of the probability bound in Theorem 5.1.

C.1 Parameters

· SEU rate per bit: \lambda_{\text{bit}} = 10^{-7}\;\text{upsets·bit}^{-1}\text{s}^{-1} (conservative for lunar surface, assuming 100 mil aluminium shielding).
· Critical bits in CBF QP solver: N_{\text{crit}} = 5000 (state values, constraint coefficients, intermediate arithmetic).
· Control cycle: T_s = 0.2\;\text{s}.
· Mission duration: T_{\text{mission}} = 10\;\text{years} = 3.15576\times 10^8\;\text{s}.
· Number of cycles: c_T = T_{\text{mission}} / T_s = 1.57788\times 10^9.

C.2 Single‑Module Critical Upset Probability per Cycle

Assuming upsets are Poisson and independent across bits, the expected number of upsets in the critical bits per second is \Lambda = \lambda_{\text{bit}} N_{\text{crit}} = 5\times 10^{-4}\;\text{s}^{-1}. Over one cycle, the expected upset count is \Lambda T_s = 10^{-4}. The probability of at least one upset in a cycle is p_{\text{upset}} = 1 - e^{-\Lambda T_s} \approx 10^{-4}. However, not every upset is critical. A conservative estimate (Aeroflex RADECS 2015 data) is that only 3.1 % of bit‑flips cause a dangerous output, hence

p_{\text{crit}} = 0.031 \times p_{\text{upset}} \approx 3.1 \times 10^{-6}.

C.3 Triple Modular Redundancy (9‑fold voting)

With three independent modules, each having internal TMR on every critical bit (hence each module's failure probability is dominated by the chance that two or more bits in the same word are corrupted simultaneously—negligible). The probability that two out of three modules produce an identical erroneous output that outvotes the correct one is (assuming independent module failures):

p_{\text{TMR}} = \binom{3}{2} p_{\text{crit}}^2 (1-p_{\text{crit}}) + p_{\text{crit}}^3 \approx 3 p_{\text{crit}}^2 = 2.88 \times 10^{-11}.

(Here we ignore the probability that all three modules fail identically, which is p_{\text{crit}}^3 and much smaller.)

C.4 Mission Failure Probability for CBF Alone

The probability that at least one TMR‑defeating event occurs over the mission is

P_{\text{fail,CBF}} = 1 - (1 - p_{\text{TMR}})^{c_T} \approx 1 - e^{-p_{\text{TMR}} c_T} = 1 - e^{-2.88\times 10^{-11}\times 1.58\times 10^9} = 1 - e^{-0.0455} \approx 0.0445.

Thus about 4.5 % risk, unacceptably high.

C.5 Fail‑Safe ROM with Independent TMR

The fail‑safe controller resides in radiation‑hardened ROM ( \lambda_{\text{ROM}} = 10^{-10}\;\text{upsets·bit}^{-1}\text{s}^{-1} ) with 2000 critical bits, TMR, and error‑detection‑and‑correction (EDAC) scrubbed every 1 s. The undetected failure probability per cycle for the ROM (after TMR and EDAC) is exceedingly small; conservative values from literature (e.g., Xilinx, 2020) give p_{\text{ROM}} \le 10^{-18} per cycle. This accounts for the probability that an upset defeats both the TMR voting and the EDAC, or causes a common‑mode failure. (Even if we relax to p_{\text{ROM}} = 10^{-14}, the product remains tiny.)

C.6 Combined System Failure Probability

The top‑level safety violation requires both the CBF TMR to fail and the fail‑safe ROM to fail (or be unable to override) in the same cycle. Because the modules are physically independent and the fail‑safe is monitored by a watchdog, these events are independent. The joint probability per cycle is

p_{\text{joint}} = p_{\text{TMR}} \times p_{\text{ROM}} < 2.88 \times 10^{-11} \times 10^{-18} = 2.88 \times 10^{-29}.

Over the mission:

P_{\text{fail}} \le c_T \times p_{\text{joint}} \approx 1.58 \times 10^9 \times 2.88 \times 10^{-29} = 4.55 \times 10^{-20}.

This is far below 10^{-9}. Even with p_{\text{ROM}} = 10^{-14}, p_{\text{joint}} = 2.88\times 10^{-25}, and P_{\text{fail}} \le 4.55\times 10^{-16}, still below 10^{-9}. The bound is robust.

C.7 Caveats (Explicit, per argumentation culture)

· The independence assumption may be violated by common‑mode failures (power glitch, clock disturbance). We partially mitigate this by separate power rails and clock trees.
· The fail‑safe ROM look‑up table correctness is assured by exhaustive testing and formal verification of each entry; this verification is outside the scope of the present thesis but is a necessary future step.
· The SEU rates are based on published data for a quiet sun; during solar particle events, rates can increase by orders of magnitude. We assume a warning system that forces the habitat into a safe, low‑power state with reduced active logic during such events—this mode is not modelled here but would rely on the same fail‑safe ROM.

