Appendix D — Dependency Diagram of Theorems

Figure D.1 (not rendered here but described) illustrates the acyclic logical dependencies among the main results:

· Theorem 2.3 (QP feasibility) depends on the PMS contract guarantee P_{\text{elec}} \ge 4.1\;\text{kW} (Lemma 4.1) and the actuator limits.
· Theorem 2.1 (CBF invariance) depends on the CBF condition (Definition 2.3).
· Theorem 3.1 (inductive invariant) is a direct consequence of Theorem 2.1.
· Theorem 4.2 (assume‑guarantee composition) depends on Lemma 4.1 (which uses the guarantees of the agents) and the individual agent contracts; it does not depend on the CBF feasibility, only on the fact that each agent’s local controller (which implements the CBF filter) satisfies its contract. This decoupling is crucial.
· Theorem 5.1 (reliability) depends only on hardware parameters.
· Finally, Theorem 6.1 (end‑to‑end certificate) combines Theorems 2.1, 2.3, 4.2, and 5.1 in a chain, with no cycles.

The diagram ensures that every assumption used in one proof is discharged by a theorem from another part without circular reasoning. This transparency was demanded by our peer‑review process and is a direct manifestation of the “sociomathematical norm” of rigorous justification.

